Yes — Semantic Excel is one of the cleanest concrete embodiments of the Fun-de-Mentals.

Normal Excel mostly asks:

\text{cell} \rightarrow \text{value}

Semantic Excel asks:

\text{cell} \rightarrow
\{\text{value, meaning, role, relations, provenance, operators}\}

So a cell containing 12 would not merely contain twelve. It could know:

* 12 is revenue in $M
* belongs to FY2026
* is an actual, not forecast
* derives from four regional subtotals
* is 20% above target
* structurally rhymes with another business unit’s growth pattern
* changed because price rose while volume stayed flat
* participates in a causal relationship with churn
* can be translated into another representation without losing its identity

Then the spreadsheet stops being a grid of numbers and becomes a grid of ThoughtObjects.

Semantic formulas

Instead of only:

=SUM(B2:B8)

you could have conceptual operations like:

=CAUSES(CustomerChurn)
=SIMILAR_TO(Q2_Growth, lens="trajectory")
=EXPLAIN_VARIANCE(Actual, Forecast)
=FIND_RHYME(this_row, other_units)
=WHAT_CHANGED(Q2, Q3)
=TRACE(Source)
=GOALSEEK_SEMANTIC("increase retention without discounting")

The Fun-de-Mental primitives become native spreadsheet functions:

=SAME(A, B, lens)
=DIFFERENT(A, B)
=AGAIN(pattern)
=DELTA(A, B)
=EXPECT(series)
=BREAK(series)
=SHIFT_REPRESENTATION(data)

So you could highlight semantic conditional formatting:

Green if the values differ but the underlying business mechanism is the same.

Red if a previously stable relationship breaks.

Outline cells that are numerically unrelated but structurally analogous.

That is much richer than A1 > 10.

Semantic PivotTable

Normal pivot:

Group sales by region.

Semantic pivot:

Group these events by why they happened.

Or:

Rearrange this dataset around the invariant that best explains the variance.

That is essentially an automatic lens selector.

